1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,164
This is EdgeGuard. It stops forklift accidents before they happen, with oneM2M and ETSI MEC.

2
00:00:07,164 --> 00:00:22,751
Workers and forklifts share the same aisles. In Korea there were over two thousand five hundred forklift casualties in two years. Proximity alarms make sites choose: set wide, trucks lose half their throughput; narrowed, collisions get through.

3
00:00:22,751 --> 00:00:35,813
EdgeGuard predicts the collision course one to three seconds ahead, on the MEC edge. It tells each worker WAIT, STEP ASIDE, or GO, and the forklift keeps its speed as long as it can still stop short of the person.

4
00:00:35,813 --> 00:00:48,962
Each warehouse zone has a MEC host, running a oneM2M MN-CSE and our MEC app. Tags and forklift modules register their own AEs. An IN-CSE in the cloud ties the zones together.

5
00:00:48,962 --> 00:00:51,462
Here it is, live.

6
00:00:51,462 --> 00:01:51,462
The worker crossing gets WAIT, until the forklift has passed, then GO. A worker standing in the lane is told to step aside. With two forklifts, GO comes only after both have passed. Every position, and every alert, is a oneM2M resource.

7
00:01:51,462 --> 00:01:53,962
Firmware in the loop.

8
00:01:53,962 --> 00:02:10,629
The ESP32 firmware runs unmodified against a real oneM2M CSE, and a phone acts as the worker tag. CI checks this on every commit.

9
00:02:10,629 --> 00:02:13,561
Mobility, through ETSI MEC.

10
00:02:13,561 --> 00:02:30,061
The MEC platform sees the forklift enter zone B. Our MEC-IPE writes the event into oneM2M, and the forklift's twin moves to zone B's CSE, with a few milliseconds of interruption, and no sample lost.

11
00:02:30,061 --> 00:02:41,340
All four ESTIMED gaps run as features: MEC interworking and orchestration, handover, federated learning with model delivery and rollback, and forklift swarm coordination.

12
00:02:41,340 --> 00:02:51,516
oneM2M does the work: SDT twins with SAREF, group fan-out, polling channels, access control with TLS, and an AI agent over MCP.

13
00:02:51,516 --> 00:03:04,201
If the network or the MEC app fails, protection is layered: stop points the truck brakes for on its own, device-to-device state, a tag that checks GO by itself, and an always-on emergency brake.

14
00:03:04,201 --> 00:03:14,923
On one thousand fresh simulated episodes, EdgeGuard had zero collisions, like the wide proximity alarm, at twice its throughput, with eighty-one percent fewer alarms.

15
00:03:14,923 --> 00:03:26,109
And on eight thousand episodes with a bad network, server restarts, low compliance and noisy positioning: zero collisions in thirty-four thousand conflicts, down from thirty.

16
00:03:26,109 --> 00:03:31,109
EdgeGuard. Safe and productive. Thank you.

